# Line endings, and one file where they are not cosmetic.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the usual Windows setting and it rewrites the working
# tree to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for Python and TypeScript, and
# fatal for `deploy/entrypoint.sh`: the API image is built with `COPY . .` from
# that working tree, so the script arrives in the image with `#!/bin/sh\r`,
# Linux looks for an interpreter literally named `sh\r`, and the container dies
# with "no such file or directory" naming the script that plainly exists.
#
# The repository blob was always LF -- CI on Linux checks out LF and builds a
# working image -- so this only ever broke a build run on a developer's machine,
# which is exactly the build `compose.yaml` runs.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same class of problem, less dramatic: a heredoc or a line continuation in a
# Dockerfile with CRLF is a parse error rather than a runtime one.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
compose.yaml text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Windows tooling, and it should stay Windows-shaped.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
